  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 147 AM CDT Tue Sep 15 2026\n\nEarly this morning, low pressure is centered near the \nManitoba/Ontario border with a cold front stretching south \nacross Minnesota into Iowa. A corridor of strong warm air \nadvection well ahead of the front is driving an ongoing line of \nshowers and thunderstorms. This activity will generally shift to\nthe northeast within the southwesterly flow, with some eastward\npropagation bringing precipitation chances close to or just \ninto portions of the Illinois River Valley during the predawn \nand early morning hours. \n\nThe front is expected to push into central Illinois this \nafternoon and stall across the region through mid to late week \nas it lays out parallel to the upper-level flow. Widespread \nshowers and thunderstorms will accompany the front today and \nWednesday, bringing localized severe weather and heavy rainfall \nrisks. Unsettled weather and high humidity will persist through \nlate week.\n\nThe parameter space will be favorable to support a few severe \nstorms this afternoon and evening. As the frontal boundary \nsettles over central Illinois this afternoon, diurnal heating \nwill produce modest instability ahead of the front, with MLCAPE \nincreasing to 1000\u20131500 J/kg. Meanwhile, deep-layer shear of \n30\u201340 kt, driven by an upper-level jet streak over the Upper \nMidwest, will intersect the stalled boundary. CSU machine \nlearning continues to paint a swath of severe wind probabilities\nof 15\u201330% across much of central Illinois. SPC maintains a \nMarginal Risk (level 1 of 5) for severe thunderstorms today.\n\nHydro concerns persist as steering flow becomes quasi-parallel \nto the frontal zone, setting up convective training. HREF mean \nQPF through Wednesday evening indicates that much of central \nIllinois north of I-72 and south of I-70 could see between \n0.5-1.5 inches of rainfall. HREF LPMM, meanwhile, indicates a \nfew isolated swaths of 2-3 inches will be possible, especially \nnear and north of the Illinois River.\n\nTemperatures are expected to warm well into the 90s across most\nof central and southeast Illinois east of the Illinois River. \nTemperatures south of I-72/Danville have around an 80\u201390% chance\nof reaching or exceeding 95 degrees this afternoon per NBM. Dew\npoints in the 50s this morning will be replaced by low to \nmid-70 dew points this afternoon. This will result in triple- \ndigit heat indices this afternoon, especially along and south of\nI-72/Danville. We may flirt with reaching Heat Advisory \ncriteria (105F), but confidence in this occurring on a \nwidespread basis is currently low and precludes extending heat \nheadlines further north into central Illinois at this time.\n\nA sharp thermal gradient will be in place over central Illinois\nacross the frontal boundary starting Wednesday. Highs north of \nthe front will remain in the upper 70s to mid-80s, while areas \nto the south reach the low to mid-90s with dew points climbing \ninto the lower 70s.\n\nThursday through Saturday: The overall synoptic pattern is slow\nto change through the end of the week into the weekend. A \nstrong upper-level high will remain anchored over the \nsoutheastern CONUS with ridging extending across the Midwest. \nThe surface frontal boundary will waffle over the region but \nremain in our general area, providing daily precipitation \nchances along with a sharp north-to-south temperature contrast \nacross the front. Temperatures in the 90s remain favored across \nsouthern portions of the forecast area, with mid-80s in the \nnorth.\n\nSunday through Tuesday: The upper ridge is progged to weaken \nlate in the weekend into early next week. To our west, a closed \nupper low will track east across the northern Great Plains into \nthe Upper Midwest. Another round of showers and thunderstorms \nwill accompany this wave Sunday, but precipitation chances and \n90-degree temperatures will eventually settle south of the area \nby Monday and Tuesday as a cooler air mass filters into the \nregion in its wake.",
